<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Omride dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 885–841 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> · Also known as House of Omri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Omride dynasty was the royal house of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a> founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omri">Omri</a> around 885 BCE and destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a>'s coup around 841 BCE. Under Omri and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a> Israel became a significant regional power, building <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a> as its capital, allying with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> and ruling Moab. Assyrian texts went on calling Israel the 'House of Omri' long after the dynasty fell.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/omride-dynasty.jpg" alt="Omride dynasty" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bukvoed · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sebastia-393.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Omrides were the fourth ruling dynasty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a>. According to the Bible its kings were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omri">Omri</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a>, Ahaziah and Jehoram, and Ahab's daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athaliah">Athaliah</a> became queen regnant of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>. The biblical account has them contending with Aram-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and Moab and allied to Judah by marriage. It records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a> killed Jehoram and his ally King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahaziah-of-judah">Ahaziah of Judah</a> and destroyed most of the remaining house, and that Athaliah survived to take the Judean throne. Modern historians date the reigns by the chronologies of William F. Albright, Edwin R. Thiele, Gershon Galil or Kenneth Kitchen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Bible portrays the Omrides as apostates who abandoned Yahwism for the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a>. Scholars have questioned this picture. Edward Lipiński suggested that the Baal of the texts was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">YHWH</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, condemned as heresy by pro-Judean authors, and the royal names Jehoram, Ahaziah and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athaliah">Athaliah</a> refer to Yahweh, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jezebel">Jezebel</a>'s seal and background point the other way. Christian Frevel argues that the Omrides, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a>, brought YHWH to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> as a patron god of the state, and that Yahwist traditions, possibly including those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-exodus">the Exodus</a>, may have been kept by the family.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesha-stele">Mesha Stele</a>, a Moabite inscription of about 840 BCE, tells of the oppression of Moab by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omri">Omri</a> king of Israel and his son, and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesha">Mesha</a>'s victories over the latter. The author of the Tel Dan Stele, usually identified as King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazael">Hazael</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, seems to claim that he himself killed the two kings, which casts doubt on the biblical account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a>. Five Assyrian records name the Land or House of Omri, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-obelisk-of-shalmaneser-iii">Black Obelisk of Shalmaneser III</a> calls Jehu a son of Omri, which may mean that he was, or styled himself, a descendant. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israel-finkelstein">Israel Finkelstein</a> holds that the Omrides built or refurbished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, Jezreel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Megiddo</a> and Hazor, and that the wealth later credited to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> was theirs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Omrides” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Assyrian scribes went on calling Israel ‘the <b>House of Omri</b>’ for well over a century, long after Jehu had wiped out Omri’s dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
